<!-- 7. OBJECTIONS -->
<h2>What I wanted to know before I ordered</h2>

<div class="note"><b>Testimonial block.</b> Five real customers, each doing one job: one aged 60+, one with mild/early-stage bags to widen the market, one who explicitly rejects skincare, one who had considered surgery, one whose spouse or colleague noticed. Real people, real permission. Run with none rather than with invented ones.</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Is this makeup? Is anyone going to be able to tell?"</p>
  <p>No, and no. It is not a cover-up and there is no colour in it — nothing is left on your skin in the morning beyond what has already absorbed. You wear them overnight. Nobody sees the product, and there is nothing to detect on your face the next day.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Is this going to turn into a routine?"</p>
  <p>Press two on before bed, peel them off in the morning, two nights a week. There is no cleanser step, no serum step, nothing to layer, and nothing that needs to live on a shelf where people can see it.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Does it hurt?"</p>
  <p>A prickle for the first minute while the cones soften, then you stop noticing. They are far too short to reach a nerve. Sharp pain is not normal — take it off.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"My bags are genetic. Dad had them, granddad had them."</p>
  <p>Then read this bit carefully, because it is the honest answer rather than the one that sells more. If the fullness is <strong>inherited fat pads</strong> — the kind visible in photos of you at twenty-five — no patch, cream or serum shifts those. Surgery does. What responds is everything else: fluid, thinning skin, lost firmness, the texture that catches hard light. Most men are a mix of both, which is why two blokes the same age get different results. If you are mostly the first kind, save your money.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Why has no doctor mentioned this to me?"</p>
  <p>Mine had not either. GP and dermatology appointments are short and triaged toward medical problems. Cosmetic delivery methods do not come up unless you raise them. It is a fifteen-minute appointment, not a conspiracy.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"I have sensitive skin / I react to things."</p>
  <p>Patch test on the inner forearm, wait 24 hours. If you have eczema, dermatitis or rosacea around the eye, or broken skin, skip it. Mild pink that fades within half an hour is expected. Swelling, itching or burning is not — stop and email them.</p>
</div>

